0. padding = In CSS, padding is the property used to create space inside an element, between its content and its border. It acts as internal "breathing room" or filler that pushes the element's boundaries outward, making the element look larger and its content easier to read. Because padding is inside the element, it adopts the element's background color or background image.The CSS Box Model ContextTo understand padding, it helps to look at how the MDN Web Docs Box Model divides every HTML element:Content: The actual text, images, or child elements.Padding: The space directly surrounding the content.Border: The outline wrapping around the content and padding.Margin: The empty space outside the border used to separate the element from its neighbors.Padding Syntax and ShorthandsYou can set padding for all sides at once using the padding shorthand property, or define individual sides independently.Individual Propertiespadding-toppadding-rightpadding-bottompadding-leftThe padding Shorthand BreakdownThe behavior of the padding shorthand changes depending on how many values you supply:1 Value: Applies to all four sides equally.csspadding: 20px; /* Top, Right, Bottom, Left = 20px */
Use code with caution.2 Values: First value handles Top/Bottom, second handles Left/Right.csspadding: 10px 20px; /* Top/Bottom = 10px, Left/Right = 20px */
Use code with caution.3 Values: Handles Top, then Left/Right, then Bottom.csspadding: 10px 20px 15px; /* Top = 10px, Left/Right = 20px, Bottom = 15px */
Use code with caution.4 Values: Moves clockwise starting from the top: Top, Right, Bottom, Left.csspadding: 10px 20px 15px 5px; /* Top = 10px, Right = 20px, Bottom = 15px, Left = 5px */
Use code with caution.
